    "description": "A documented onboarding process model for integrating a new employee at an SME: key steps, practical application, and the mistake to avoid.",
    "h1": "Onboarding Process: A Model for SMEs",
    "bodyHtml": "<h1>Onboarding Process: A Model for SMEs</h1>\n<p>The <strong>onboarding process</strong>, or integration process, describes how a company brings a new employee on board, from the signing of the contract to full autonomy in the role. A successful onboarding process is not a matter of friendliness: it is a documented, repeatable process that turns individual knowledge into company capability. Without it, every new hire starts from scratch and depends on the availability \u2014 or the memory \u2014 of whoever \"knows how things are done.\"</p>\n\n<h2>The onboarding process in four stages</h2>\n<h3>1. Before arrival (preboarding)</h3>\n<p>Workstation, access, equipment, and documents are ready before the first day. A plan for the first few weeks is defined, with a designated point of contact. What is prepared in advance doesn't need to be caught up on at the last minute.</p>\n<h3>2. The first days</h3>\n<p>Welcome, introduction to the team and how things work, clear goals for the first few weeks. The point isn't to convey everything at once, but to give the new hire bearings and a first concrete scope of work.</p>\n<h3>3. Skill-building</h3>\n<p>Training on the tasks and processes of the role, with regular check-ins. This is where documented procedures make the difference: they let the new hire learn without monopolizing an experienced colleague.</p>\n<h3>4. Confirmation</h3>\n<p>A check-in at thirty, sixty, and ninety days verifies autonomy, adjusts what needs adjusting, and formally closes out the onboarding. What matters is less how well someone fits in socially than their real ability to handle the role.</p>\n\n<h2>When to formalize the onboarding process in an SME?</h2>\n<p>As long as an SME hires one person a year, informal integration gets by. The process becomes necessary once hiring picks up, roles become interdependent, or a departure takes with it knowledge that no one wrote down. Documenting onboarding means treating operational knowledge as a company asset rather than an individual's property \u2014 the same logic as the <strong>Two-Layer Model</strong>, which distinguishes what is decided from what is executed, and makes execution transferable.</p>\n\n<h2>The common mistake</h2>\n<p>The most common mistake is reducing onboarding to the first day: a warm welcome, then nothing structured afterward. The new hire is left to fend for themselves and learns by osmosis, at the pace of whoever happens to be available. The result: slow skill-building, avoidable mistakes, and sometimes early departure. Onboarding unfolds over several weeks and ends with a check on autonomy, not with a team lunch.</p>\n\n<h2>Document your key processes</h2>\n<p>Want to know which processes should be documented as a priority in your company? Our <a href=\"/diagnostic\">free diagnostic</a> identifies, in forty-five minutes, the points where knowledge depends too heavily on individuals.</p>\n\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>What is an onboarding process?</h3>\n<p>It's the documented way a company integrates a new employee, from the signed contract to full autonomy in the role.</p>\n<h3>How long does an onboarding process last?</h3>\n<p>It generally spans several weeks to a few months, with check-ins at thirty, sixty, and ninety days to confirm autonomy.</p>\n<h3>Why document the onboarding process in an SME?</h3>\n<p>So that knowledge of the role becomes a transferable company asset, instead of depending on a colleague's memory.</p>",
