    "h1": "Supply Chain Consultant: When and Why",
    "bodyHtml": "<h1>Supply Chain Consultant: When and Why</h1>\n<p>A <strong>supply chain consultant</strong> helps a company better manage the flow that runs from customer demand to delivery: forecasting, planning, inventory, procurement, capacity. Their role isn't to replace the teams, but to give them a method and an outside perspective where habit has ended up masking the problems. For an SME, the real question isn't \"do we need a consultant?\" but \"at what point, and for what purpose?\"</p>\n\n<h2>The signals that indicate the right moment</h2>\n<p>A few situations come up again and again.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stockouts and unsold inventory coexist: demand is not under control.</li>\n<li>Decisions are made under pressure, with no plan shared between sales and operations.</li>\n<li>The company is growing, and its processes, inherited from a smaller era, can no longer keep up.</li>\n<li>A major project (new ERP, new site, strong growth) requires a skill the team doesn't yet have in-house.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In these cases, the intervention is justified not by the size of the company, but by the gap between the complexity reached and the methods in place.</p>\n\n<h2>What a supply chain consultant really delivers</h2>\n<p>A good engagement leaves the company more self-sufficient than it found it. It brings a structured method, a factual diagnosis of the situation, and above all a transfer of skill: the teams must be able to run the setup on their own once the engagement ends. It also brings a neutral perspective, free of internal habits and politics, which makes it possible to name problems the organization had come to accept as normal. This is the distinction carried by the <strong>Two-Layer Model</strong> \u2014 separating the decision layer from the execution layer \u2014 applied to the engagement itself: helping leadership decide better, and operations execute reliably, without creating a permanent dependency on the consultant.</p>\n\n<h2>The common mistake</h2>\n<p>The costliest mistake is hiring a supply chain consultant for a deliverable \u2014 a report, a tool \u2014 instead of a capability. A thick report that ends up in a drawer changes nothing; what actually changes something is a team that has learned to manage differently. Before hiring, it's therefore better to clarify the expected outcome in terms of capability gained, not just documents produced.</p>\n\n<h2>Take stock with no commitment</h2>\n<p>Unsure about the value and scope of bringing someone in? Our forty-five-minute <a href=\"/diagnostic\">free diagnostic</a> takes an initial, factual look at your supply chain maturity, with no commitment.</p>\n\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>What does a supply chain consultant do?</h3>\n<p>They help the company manage the flow from demand to delivery \u2014 forecasting, planning, inventory, procurement \u2014 by bringing a method and an outside perspective.</p>\n<h3>When should an SME hire a supply chain consultant?</h3>\n<p>When the company's complexity outgrows its current methods: simultaneous stockouts and unsold inventory, decisions made under pressure, growth, or a major project.</p>\n<h3>Does a supply chain consultant create dependency?</h3>\n<p>A good engagement does the opposite: it transfers the skill so the teams can run the setup on their own after the engagement ends.</p>",
