    "h1": "Supply Chain Training In-House",
    "bodyHtml": "<h1>Supply Chain Training In-House</h1>\n<p><strong>Supply chain training</strong> delivered in-house, or \"in-company,\" means training a team directly on the methods and processes of its own context, rather than in a classroom with generic case studies. The difference is decisive: instead of learning concepts in the abstract, participants work on their real flows, their real constraints, and their real numbers. The skill acquired stays within the company and applies as soon as the next day.</p>\n\n<h2>Who supply chain training is for</h2>\n<p>This format is aimed primarily at SME teams running operations without having gone through structured training: sales, purchasing, production, or logistics managers, as well as executives who want to speak the same language as their teams. The goal isn't to train specialists, but to give generalists the reference points that make their decisions more sound.</p>\n\n<h2>How supply chain training unfolds</h2>\n<h3>1. Scoping</h3>\n<p>The skills actually useful to the context are identified: forecasting, inventory management, planning, process improvement. Whatever doesn't serve the company today is set aside.</p>\n<h3>2. Workshops on your own cases</h3>\n<p>Skill-building happens through workshops applied to your own processes, alternating between method input and immediate hands-on practice. Learning happens by doing, on the company's own material.</p>\n<h3>3. Anchoring</h3>\n<p>Lasting reference points are left behind \u2014 materials, procedures, indicators \u2014 so that the skill survives the training and doesn't depend on participants' memory.</p>\n\n<h2>When to favor in-house training in an SME?</h2>\n<p>In-company training makes sense as soon as several people need to build the same skills, or when operational knowledge rests on a handful of individuals. Training the team means turning individual knowledge into collective capability \u2014 the \"execution\" layer of the <strong>Two-Layer Model</strong>, made solid and shared. A decision made well at the top is only worth as much as the team executing it masters the methods behind it.</p>\n\n<h2>The common mistake</h2>\n<p>The common mistake is choosing generic training, disconnected from the context: participants come out with concepts they don't know how to apply to their daily work. The other pitfall is training without anchoring anything \u2014 no materials, no procedures \u2014 so the skill evaporates within a few weeks. Useful training is measured by what it leaves behind in the company, not by the number of hours delivered.</p>\n\n<h2>Build a tailored skill-building plan</h2>\n<p>Want to identify the skills to strengthen as a priority in your team? Our forty-five-minute <a href=\"/diagnostic\">free diagnostic</a> helps frame a skill-building plan tailored to your context.</p>\n\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>What is in-house supply chain training?</h3>\n<p>It's in-company training that builds a team's skills directly on the methods and processes of its own context, rather than on generic case studies.</p>\n<h3>Who is in-company supply chain training for?</h3>\n<p>SME teams running operations \u2014 sales, purchasing, production, logistics \u2014 and executives who want to share the same methodological language.</p>\n<h3>What's the difference from a classic classroom course?</h3>\n<p>In-house training relies on your own flows, constraints, and numbers; the skill acquired stays within the company and applies immediately.</p>",
