    "title": "Safety Stock: How to Calculate It in an SME",
    "description": "What is safety stock for, and how do you calculate it? The factors that determine it, the logic of the calculation, and the common mistake to avoid in an SME.",
    "h1": "Safety Stock: How to Calculate It",
    "bodyHtml": "<h1>Safety Stock: How to Calculate It</h1>\n<p><strong>Safety stock</strong> is the quantity of goods kept in reserve to absorb uncertainty: demand stronger than expected, a supplier lead time that stretches out. It's a buffer that protects the service level when reality departs from the forecast. Too low, it exposes the company to stockouts; too high, it ties up cash and space. Calculating safety stock means finding the right balance between these two risks.</p>\n\n<h2>What determines safety stock</h2>\n<p>Three factors drive it, regardless of the formula used.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Demand variability</strong>: the more irregular sales are, the higher the buffer needs to be.</li>\n<li><strong>Lead time and its reliability</strong>: a long or uncertain lead time increases the safety stock required.</li>\n<li><strong>The targeted service level</strong>: the more the company wants to reduce the risk of stockout, the larger the buffer \u2014 protecting against the last few percentage points of risk costs proportionally more.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>The logic of the calculation</h2>\n<p>The common method links these factors: a coefficient tied to the targeted service level, demand variability, and lead time are combined to obtain the buffer quantity. The essential idea to remember isn't the formula itself, but its logic: safety stock increases with uncertainty (in both demand and lead time) and with the service requirement. A spreadsheet is enough to apply it item by item; what matters is using real variability data, not a flat margin applied identically across the whole catalog.</p>\n\n<h2>When to pay attention to safety stock in an SME</h2>\n<p>As soon as an SME manages several SKUs and suffers stockouts or overstocks, safety stock deserves to be calculated rather than set \"by feel.\" Sizing it correctly is part of the \"execution\" layer of the <strong>Two-Layer Model</strong>: once the service policy is decided at the top, safety stock is the concrete setting that makes it real on the ground.</p>\n\n<h2>The common mistake</h2>\n<p>The most widespread mistake is applying a uniform percentage to every item \u2014 for instance, \"two weeks of stock\" across the board. But a regular item and an erratic item don't need the same buffer. The result: stable products get over-stocked while volatile products still run short. The other mistake is forgetting the variability of supplier lead time, often more decisive than that of demand.</p>\n\n<h2>Check your inventory levels</h2>\n<p>Suspect your safety stock levels are poorly set? Our forty-five-minute <a href=\"/diagnostic\">free diagnostic</a> examines the logic behind your levels, with no commitment.</p>\n\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>What is safety stock?</h3>\n<p>It's a quantity kept in reserve to absorb uncertainty in demand and lead times, and to protect the service level when reality departs from the forecast.</p>\n<h3>How do you calculate safety stock?</h3>\n<p>By combining the targeted service level, demand variability, and lead time; the buffer increases with uncertainty and with the service requirement.</p>\n<h3>What is the most common mistake with safety stock?</h3>\n<p>Applying a uniform percentage to every item, without accounting for each SKU's own variability or the supplier lead time.</p>",
